Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. STUDY - NIGHT

Rain beats against the windowpane. A cozy fire crackles in the hearth. 

(SOUND: Rain drumming on glass, fire crackling)

NARRATOR (60s, warm eyes, wearing a tweed jacket) sits in a worn leather armchair. He holds a small, faded sprig of dried holly.

NARRATOR
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid. On Christmas morning, when he sat wedged in the top of the Boy's stocking, with a sprig of holly between his paws, the effect was charming.

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

Sunlight streams over a mountain of torn tissue paper and unwrapped parcels. 

(SOUND: Rustling paper, a clockwork mouse whirring across the floor)

THE BOY (6, wearing a white nightshirt) sits on the rug. He holds the VELVETEEN RABBIT. The Rabbit is fat and bunchy, with a spotted brown and white coat, real thread whiskers, and ears lined with pink sateen. 

The Boy hugs the Rabbit tightly. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. And then Aunts and Uncles came to dinner, and in the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

The Boy drops the Rabbit into a dark corner of the toy cupboard and runs out of the room.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight cuts across the quiet toy cupboard. The Velveteen Rabbit sits timidly in the shadows. 

(SOUND: A clock ticks steadily in the hall)

MODEL BOAT (chipped paint, missing a sail) leans against a wooden block, looking down at the Rabbit.

MODEL BOAT
My rigging is entirely to scale. A marvel of modern technical engineering. Sawdust is quite out-of-date, you know. It should never be mentioned in modern circles.

The Velveteen Rabbit shrinks back. 

Across the room, SKIN HORSE rests near the nursery fender. He is so old that his brown coat is bald in patches, showing the seams underneath, and most of the hairs in his tail have been pulled out. 

The Velveteen Rabbit hops over and lies down beside him.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA (brisk, wearing a starched apron) swoops into the room like a great wind. 

(SOUND: Heavy footsteps, cupboard doors banging open)

The Boy sits up in bed, looking around frantically.

THE BOY
I can't find my china dog!

Nana sighs, too hurried to hunt for china dogs. She marches to the toy cupboard, reaches in, and drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She thrusts the Rabbit into the Boy's arms. The Boy immediately clasps him close under his little warm chin.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Springtime. The Boy pushes a wooden wheelbarrow across the sunlit lawn. 

(SOUND: Wooden wheel squeaking, birds chirping)

The Velveteen Rabbit rides inside. He is noticeably shabbier now--his tail unsewn, his fur matted, and the pink rubbed completely off his nose. 

The Boy lifts the Rabbit and dives with him into a burrow dug in the flower bed. They tumble in the dirt.

Nana marches out onto the lawn, carrying a candle in the gathering dusk. She spots the Boy and snatches the muddy Rabbit from him, rubbing the toy roughly with a corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

The Boy stretches out his hands.

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Velveteen Rabbit's boot-button eyes seem to shine. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he was happy. The nursery magic had happened to him. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Tall bracken sways in the fading summer light. The Velveteen Rabbit lies alone in a little nest of grass. 

(SOUND: Leaves rustling, soft padding of paws)

Two wild FURRY RABBITS creep out of the shadows. They are brand-new, with no seams showing at all. Their noses twitch.

FURRY RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

FURRY RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything. 

The Furry Rabbit gives a big hop sideways and stands on his hind legs.

FURRY RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

FURRY RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit sits perfectly still. The back of him is made all in one piece, like a pincushion. 

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Furry Rabbit stretches out his neck and looks closely.

FURRY RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs! 

The Furry Rabbit begins to laugh, whirling around in a dance.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

FURRY RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The Furry Rabbit stops dancing and comes very close. His long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and jumps backwards.

FURRY RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

(SOUND: Heavy footsteps approaching)

The Boy runs past the bracken. With a flash of white tails, the two wild rabbits disappear into the brush. 

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only the little ants run to and fro. The Velveteen Rabbit is all alone.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A dim night-light burns. The Boy tosses under heavy blankets, his face flushed bright red with fever. 

(SOUND: Labored, ragged breathing, tense low music)

The Velveteen Rabbit--now very old, his whiskers loved off, his brown spots faded to grey--is tucked tightly under the Boy's chin. The Rabbit does not stir.

DOCTOR (stern, carrying a black bag) stands over the bed with Nana.

DOCTOR
The fever has turned. He will recover. But the room must be disinfected. 

Nana points to the bedclothes.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

A canvas sack sits in the dirt among old picture-books and rubbish. 

(SOUND: Wind howling, dry leaves scraping the ground)

The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles his head out of the untied sack. He shivers in the cold. His coat is thin and threadbare. 

He looks toward the thicket of raspberry canes in the distance. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this? 

A tear, a real tear, trickles down the Rabbit's shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: A soft, shimmering magical chime)

Where the tear falls, a mysterious flower grows out of the dirt. It has emerald leaves and a blossom like a golden cup. The blossom opens.

FAIRY steps out. She is the loveliest fairy in the world, wearing a dress of pearl and dew-drops, with flowers in her hair. 

She gathers the Velveteen Rabbit up in her arms and kisses his damp nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I... I think I have seen your face before.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moonlight illuminates an open glade. The wild rabbits dance on the velvet grass. 

(SOUND: Crickets chirping, magical sweeping music)

The Fairy lands in the center of the ring, holding the Velveteen Rabbit. The wild rabbits stop dancing and stare.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit one last time and puts him down on the grass.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Fairy vanishes. 

The Velveteen Rabbit sits quite still. He remembers his lack of hind legs. He doesn't want to move. 

Something tickles his nose. Without thinking, he lifts a hind toe to scratch it. 

He looks down. Instead of dingy velveteen, he has brown fur, soft and shiny. His ears twitch by themselves. He has actual hind legs.

He gives one massive leap into the air. 

(SOUND: Joyful, soaring orchestral swell)

He springs about the turf, jumping sideways and whirling round, finally at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Spring sunlight filters through the trees. The Boy, looking healthy and bright, plays in the brush behind the house. 

(SOUND: Gentle acoustic guitar, birds singing)

Two rabbits creep out from the bracken and peek at him. One is brown all over. 

The other is the REAL RABBIT--brown fur, soft and shiny, but with strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted. 

The Real Rabbit stares at the Boy with familiar round black eyes.

THE BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Real Rabbit twitches his nose, then turns and bounds joyfully into the deep forest.

INT. STUDY - NIGHT

The fire has burned down to glowing embers. 

(SOUND: Fire crackling softly)

The Narrator smiles, looking down at the dried sprig of holly in his hand. He gently sets it on the mantelpiece.
